            <ArticleTitle>Selection of local anesthetics in dentistry: clinical impression versus scientific assessment.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>21-4</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Since its introduction to in the United States about six years ago, 4% articaine with 1:200,000 epinephrine has been used by dentists as a local anesthetic agent. This article reviews three claims that have been advanced regarding articaine: high diffusibility, reduced incidence of failure in block injections, and effectiveness in achieving anesthesia when used in cases involving irreversible pulpitis.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
